Hello Martina,

Thanks for the response. It is very useful that network compiles a
list of vertex.names when you input the data this way; it brings some
piece of mind also that the numerical ID codes are attached to the
proper names, and saves extra work since the data may come naturally
with names. Network is assigning these codes somehow, presumably
sorting the data in a way similar to the factor process. My input data
is sorted in the exact same way that the as.edgelist.sna command
outputs the data, so I was hoping this would be straightforward. I
will try feeding it the encoded data however as you suggest and see if
this solves the problem. I used the following somewhat ugly loop to
match each element, and that worked, but a more convenient solution,
and some clarity on the order in which the edges are stored, would be
welcome. Use the %e% operator is certainly convenient, so I hope to
get it up and running!

for(i in 1:(crnet$gal$mnext-1))
crnet$mel[[i]]$atl$ew<-cr[which(crnet$val[[crnet$mel[[i]]$outl]][[2]]==cr[,1]&crnet$val[[crnet$mel[[i]]$inl]][[2]]==cr[,2]),3]

Best,
Brooks

