 From looking at the code, it appears you need also need to tell it to 
not ignore the edge weights, and ideally name the edge attributes you 
are adding:

crnet<-network(cr,directed=F,matrix.type='edgelist',ignore.eval=FALSE,names.eval='publication_count')


The docs should probably be improved to make this clearer. I was not 
aware that building networks with non-numeric edgelists was even 
possible until you asked this question.


>
> Au Secours! The attribute vector is different than the vector in the
> original data? There must be something I'm missing, but it feels like a
> bug. A problem with how I am sorting the data on input?

I can reproduce your issue with a small synthetic example.  You clearly 
can't know in what order the edges will  to safely use direct edge 
attribute assignment when you are using character (instead of numeric) 
ids on the edges.

  It seems that sorting must be part of the explanation. From looking at 
the code, it appears that when you give it non-numeric ids it is doing 
some sorting before converting them to numeric.  This may be complicated 
by the dataframe's numeric expression of the factor levels which seems 
to be independent of the input order in the data frame. I don't have 
enough brain at the moment to figure out how this is going wrong. 
hopefly ignore.eval=FALSE will solve your issue.


