On Thu, Apr 4, 2013 at 9:12 PM, Sam Stewart <samstewart11@gmail.com> wrote:
> I believe that the problem is that the network.layout.* functions don't
> accept edge-values as arguments.

Hi,

Do you want edge values to affect network layout (vertex placement) or
the width of the edges?

Others will correct me if I'm wrong, but I don't think that the
network layout functions as currently implemented in network or sna
packages allow for using edge values as "weights".

If you need to use a "weighted layout" you might use one of the
layouts implemented in 'igraph' package, for example (assuming that
igraph is installed):

library(network)
set.seed(11)
X = matrix(floor(runif(25,0,5)),nrow=5,byrow=T)
Xnet = network(X,matrix.type='adjacency')
set.edge.value(Xnet,'value',X)

# Install intergraph package form R-Forge
# install.packages("intergraph", repos="http://R-Forge.R-project.org")
library(intergraph)
g <- asIgraph(Xnet)

# in weighted layout:
# larger weight = stronger attraction force between vertexes
lay <- igraph::layout.fruchterman.reingold(g, weights=igraph::E(g)$value)
# use statnet function to plot the network using the layout computed with igraph
plot.network(Xnet, coord=lay)

