Dear all,

I would like to ask if I can compare the magnitude of coefficients across fitted ergm models on different samples (e.g., is there more or less ethnic homophily in class room A compared to class room B?).

I know from Mood (2010), that comparing coefficients across models, groups and samples is problematic as the magnitude of a coefficient depends on the explained variance within logistic regression models (the smaller the residual variance in a model, the larger the coefficients get). I've learned that ergms are like  logistic regression models, so does this problem also apply to ergms? For example, if I'd compare coefficients in network A and B,  is the difference in coefficient size then due to a stronger tendency to befriend same-ethnic alters in one of the networks,  or because the model does  a better job in predicting friendship in one of the networks? Or am I safe comparing coefficients across networks just as they are?

If this problem does apply to ergms, could somebody give me a hint towards a possible solution?  For example, do I need to somehow standardize the coefficients so that I cancel out these 'rescaling effects' or use average marginal effects instead of log odds?

Many thanks in advance!

Regards,

