Hello,

What is the precise definition of "incidence matrix" in the context of statnet/network library?

The most common definition I was familiar with is a matrix indicating the relationship between edges and vertices: element (i,j) will be 1 if edge j is connected to vertex i.  The following line in the docs (of network.incidence) suggests that statnet uses the same definition:

"Incidence matrices should contain one row per vertex, with one column per edge."

How ever that is not what as.matrix.network.incidence actually gives me. This is what I get with statnet:

> n <- network.initialize(6, directed=F)
> add.edge(n, c(1,2), c(2,3))
> as.matrix.network.adjacency(n)
  1 2 3 4 5 6
1 0 1 0 0 0 0
2 1 0 1 0 0 0
3 0 1 0 0 0 0
4 0 0 0 0 0 0
5 0 0 0 0 0 0
6 0 0 0 0 0 0

The adjacency matrix is what I expect, so the graph that was created is indeed what I wanted to create.

> as.matrix.network.edgelist(n)
     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    1    2
[2,]    2    3
attr(,"n")
[1] 6
attr(,"vnames")
[1] 1 2 3 4 5 6

The edge list looks good too.

> as.matrix.network.incidence(n)
     [,1]
[1,]    1
[2,]    1
[3,]    1
[4,]    0
[5,]    0
[6,]    0

This is unexpected for me.  Why does the incidence matrix have a single column only?  I would have expected the following:

     [,1] [,2]
[1,]    1   0
[2,]    1   1
[3,]    0   1
[4,]    0   0
[5,]    0   0
[6,]    0   0

What am I misunderstanding?  I'm sure I must be misunderstanding something (I'm new to both R and statnet).

Thank you for any clarifications in advance,
Szabolcs Horv?t



From szhorvat at nd.edu  Mon Apr 22 10:06:58 2013
