Hello,

I have a few questions about writing custom terms for ergm using the ergm.userterms template.  I went through the documentation and I have an (I believe) working implementation, but I still have a few questions remaining:


QUESTION 1:

The tutorial shows a C function defined as

CHANGESTAT_FN(d_something) { }

If I look at the ergm sources, the terms have two corresponding functions, defined with D_CHANGESTAT_FN and S_CHANGESTAT_FN.  What are these two, and how are they different from CHANGESTAT_FN?  It seems S_CHANGESTAT_FN is for calculating the initial value in CHANGE_STAT, for example:

D_CHANGESTAT_FN(d_edges) {
  int edgeflag, i;
  Vertex tail, head;
  
  /* *** don't forget tail -> head */    
  CHANGE_STAT[0] = 0.0;
  for (i=0; i < ntoggles; i++)
    {
      edgeflag = IS_OUTEDGE(tail = TAIL(i), head = HEAD(i));
      CHANGE_STAT[0] += edgeflag ? - 1 : 1;
      TOGGLE_IF_MORE_TO_COME(i);
    }
  UNDO_PREVIOUS_TOGGLES(i);
}

S_CHANGESTAT_FN(s_edges) {
  CHANGE_STAT[0] = N_EDGES;
}

This example is copied from the ergm sources.  I tried to re-implement it (as an exercise) like this:

CHANGESTAT_FN(d_edge2) {
  int i;

  ZERO_ALL_CHANGESTATS(i);
  FOR_EACH_TOGGLE(i) {
    if (IS_OUTEDGE(TAIL(i), HEAD(i))) {
      CHANGE_STAT[0] += -1;
    }  
    else {
      CHANGE_STAT[0] += 1;
    }
    TOGGLE_IF_MORE_TO_COME(i);
  }
  UNDO_PREVIOUS_TOGGLES(i);
}

If I understand ZERO_ALL_CHANGESTATS right, my version starts with a value of 0, while the ergm version starts with the actual edge count.  Why are both acceptable?  Is it because what the algorithm uses is really just the ratio of the exponential terms (exp(a*edges_after) / exp(a*edges_before))?


QUESTION 2:

Is it okay to use N_EDGES inside FOR_EACH_TOGGLE?  Does the edge number returned by N_EDGES get updated during toggling edges?  Is it correct to say that N_EDGES, when used inside of FOR_EACH_TOGGLE, will return the number of edges present before the currently considered toggle is executed (but taking into account all previous toggles)?

Or does N_EDGES stay unchanged during a single execution of the d_something function?


QUESTION 3:

What the ergm() R function does is that it finds \theta so that the average of the network statistic g_1 over all graphs is going to be the prescribed value (target.stats) if we assign the following probability to each graph: exp(\theta g_1)/k(\theta)

I'm using the notations from the ergm tutorial.

Is it possible to use the same model, i.e. assign probabilities exp(\theta g_1)/k(\theta) to each graph, but instead of fitting g_1, fit some other quantity g_2 instead?  I.e. find \theta so that the mean value of g_2 will be what I want, but still use g_1 to assign probabilities to each graph.

Can ergm be extended by users to make this possible?

(I can see how this request might seem strange, but this is indeed what I need, and I would like to know if I can use ergm, or I need to write my own implementation.)

Thank you for any replies in advance!

